.TH SWM 1 "September 2026" "swm" "swm Manual"
.SH NAME
swm - static X11 window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B swm
is a small, configurable X11 window manager designed around a simple idea:
the window manager should manage windows and otherwise stay out of the way.

It provides tiled and monocle layouts, floating windows, tags, fullscreen
support, multi-monitor support, and the usual collection of X11 protocols
required to make a desktop behave approximately as expected.

The implementation is intentionally small and uses traditional Xlib
interfaces. There are no layers of abstraction whose primary purpose is
explaining another layer of abstraction.

.PP
.B swm
was reconstructed from a behavioral specification and implemented
independently. The project makes extensive use of AI-assisted development,
which means that some portions of the source were produced considerably
faster than the arguments about whether they should have been produced.

.SH FEATURES
.TP
.B Tags
Windows can be organized into multiple tags and viewed independently or
in combination.

.TP
.B Tiling
Windows can be arranged automatically using the configured tiling layout.

.TP
.B Monocle
A single window can occupy the available workspace, because sometimes one
window is enough.

.TP
.B Floating
Windows may be moved and resized manually when automatic arrangement is
not appropriate.

.TP
.B Fullscreen
Windows may be displayed fullscreen when requested or configured.

.TP
.B Multi-monitor
Multiple monitors are supported through the X11 monitor infrastructure.

.TP
.B Xinerama
Optional Xinerama support may be enabled at build time.

.TP
.B ICCCM and EWMH
.B swm
implements the relevant parts of ICCCM and EWMH required for normal
interaction with X11 applications and desktop environments.

.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is compiled into
.B swm .
The default configuration is provided in
.B config.def.h .

To customize the window manager, copy or modify the configuration as
appropriate and rebuild.

.PP
The configuration controls, among other things, tags, layouts, colors,
fonts, key bindings, mouse bindings, rules, and other window-management
behavior.

.SH BUILDING
Edit
.B config.mk
if build options need to be changed, then run:

.PP
.nf
.RS
make
.RE
.fi

.PP
To install:

.PP
.nf
.RS
make install
.RE
.fi

.PP
By default, the executable is installed as:

.PP
.nf
.RS
/usr/local/bin/swm
.RE
.fi

.PP
The manual page is installed as:

.PP
.nf
.RS
/usr/local/share/man/man1/swm.1
.RE
.fi

.SH SOURCE FILES
.TP
.B swm.c
Main window manager implementation.

Yes, it is still called
.B swm.c .
The name survived the project rename and has therefore become historical
infrastructure.

.TP
.B drw.c
Drawing implementation.

.TP
.B drw.h
Drawing interface.

.TP
.B util.c
Utility functions.

.TP
.B util.h
Utility interface.

.TP
.B config.def.h
Default configuration.

.TP
.B config.mk
Build configuration.

.TP
.B Makefile
Build and installation rules.

.TP
.B transient.c
Standalone transient-window test client.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B swm
uses the X11 environment provided by the running X server.

The
.B DISPLAY
environment variable determines which X display is used.

.SH SIGNALS
.B swm
may be restarted or otherwise controlled according to the signal handling
implemented by the current build.

See the source and configuration for the exact behavior.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/swm
The installed window manager executable.

.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/swm.1
This manual page.

.TP
.I config.h
The local build configuration.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR X (7),
.BR Xlib (3)

.SH NOTES
.B swm
is intentionally minimal.

This does not necessarily mean that every part of it is simple.

The distinction is important.

.PP
The project is AI-assisted. Code, documentation, and design decisions may
therefore have passed through several rounds of machine-generated
confidence before reaching the human responsible for the repository.

The human remains the final authority, which is fortunate for everyone
involved.

.SH BUGS
